<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Federated Malay States</h1><p class="st-meta">1895 CE – 1946 CE · capital Kuala Lumpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British-controlled federation of four Malay sultanates, the economic heart of colonial Malaya built on tin and rubber.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> had placed residents at the courts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selangor-sultanate">Selangor Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pahang-sultanate">Pahang Sultanate</a> and the states of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/negeri-sembilan">Negeri Sembilan</a> from 1874 onwards. In 1895 these were combined into the Federated Malay States, under a Resident-General in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a>, the first being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frank-swettenham">Frank Swettenham</a>, to unify administration, finance and railways.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans kept their thrones and authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Malay custom, but British officials ran government. Chinese-run <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mines and European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> estates worked by Indian labourers made the federation one of the most profitable parts of the empire. Railways, roads and towns spread, and a Federal Council was set up in 1909.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the states from 1942 to 1945. In 1946 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> merged them with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/unfederated-malay-states">Unfederated Malay States</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-of-wales-island">Penang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malayan-union">Malayan Union</a>, replaced in 1948 by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federated-malay-states.jpg" alt="Federated Malay States" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malaya_(1896%E2%80%931950).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1895 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuala Lumpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frank Swettenham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Buddhism, Hinduism, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, English, Chinese, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>FMS</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=federated-malay-states" aria-label="Federated Malay States on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7503 2060 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Federated Malay States lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7823 2316L7870 2303M7823 2316L7799 2255M7823 2316L7810 2309M7823 2316L7836 2333M7823 2316L4997 651M7823 2316L7823 2316M7823 2316L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7870 2303h0M7799 2255h0M7810 2309h0M7836 2333h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7823 2316h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuala Lumpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=federated-malay-states">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
